I agree that the mindless sacrificing that is currently possible, 
especially for a wizard with the "create monster" spell, is overpowered. 
This opens the way for sacrifices that really don't mean anything, as 
you noticed.

A much simpler solotion would be, imho, to have the game record the 
origin of a monster: whether it originated by random creation or by 
magic (create monster).

Another, even more restricting way, would be to limit sacrifices to ONE 
of every creature type. For example, you could only gain benifits from 
sacrificing goblins *once*.

Also, once you've sacrificed a "higher level" creature, a minor version 
of the same class of monsters wouldn't gain you a benifit anymore. For 
example, once you've sacrificed a goblin wizard, an ordinary goblin 
wouldn't benefit you any more. By "skipping" a lower type of the same 
class, the higher level type would gain you the value of the two 
combined.

